Publish S6SY761 touch reports on /dev/kebab_touch

This kernel has no HID stack to deliver a touch to: IOHIDFamily is a
separate Apple kext and none of its classes are in this tree (no
IOHIDSystem, IOHIDEventService, IOHIDEvent or IOHIDDevice sources), there
is no evdev, and the build has no KXLD so a kext cannot be loaded into it
either. The only user-readable mechanism that does exist is devfs plus
cdevsw, which is what this patch uses: decoded reports go into a static
queue and read(2) on /dev/kebab_touch returns them as 48-byte records. The
console dump stays the primary channel because userland on this port
cannot open the node yet.

Coordinates are now converted instead of printed raw. One function maps
the sensor's 1079x2399 maximum indices onto the 1080x2400 panel one to one
and adds the logical display's (22,18) presentation origin, so a touch
lands where backboardd lays it out on the 1125x2436 display. A sample
above the advertised maximum is clamped to the panel edge and flagged
rather than extrapolated past the panel.

The queue has one producer (the poll thread) and one consumer (read), a
mutex for the two, and a fixed array for storage. With nobody reading, the
oldest record is overwritten and counted, so an absent consumer costs
history, never memory. A boot-time selftest fills the ring three times
without a reader, checks that exactly 64 records survive with 128 counted
drops, drains them in order, and prints the result.

New boot arguments: kebab_touch_dump=1 prints the decoded object list
behind every touch-down, and kebab_touch_move_ms=N sets the minimum
interval between move lines (default 200 ms, 0 disables them; down and up
always print).

